00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.636
Thanks, Jan. Hi everyone. My name is Ney and I lead the enterprise
data warehouse and data architecture teams at Grab.

00:15:02.476 --> 00:15:07.876
Grab is a fairly household name here in Southeast Asia,
but for the uninitiated, we are a super app.

00:15:08.116 --> 00:15:14.276
We provide parcel delivery, food delivery,
mobility and fintech services amongst a host of other things.

00:15:14.276 --> 00:15:17.436
So data and AI has been an integral part of how we've grown.

00:15:18.116 --> 00:15:24.236
Every time you book a ride, every time you make a food order,
the allocation, the pricing, the ETA prediction,

00:15:24.356 --> 00:15:26.516
everything is powered by AI models.

00:15:27.716 --> 00:15:33.756
We've got more than a thousand of these already in production,
and we keep adding more and more as we evolve.

00:15:33.756 --> 00:15:41.796
And speaking of evolving with the new Agentic AI world, we are now
innovating and making these workflows more and more Agentic.

00:15:44.836 --> 00:15:49.036
If you had a chance to catch our annual
product launch event last month,

00:15:49.036 --> 00:15:56.356
we launched 13 AI powered experiences which included AI assistants
for our merchants, for our consumers, drivers alike.

00:15:56.716 --> 00:16:01.636
We introduced local life. We spoke about
personalized travel experiences and much more.

00:16:01.716 --> 00:16:04.596
But while agents get all the glory,

00:16:04.596 --> 00:16:10.396
there's a lot of foundational work that is required
to ensure your agents can do what you want them to do.

00:16:10.396 --> 00:16:16.676
The three broad pillars being platforms and tooling,
data practices and AI enablement for everyone.

00:16:18.116 --> 00:16:21.876
Before we dive into all three of those,
let's look at the scale that we operate at.

00:16:21.876 --> 00:16:25.716
Because models and tooling is commoditized
now and available to everybody.

00:16:25.716 --> 00:16:29.636
But at Grab, we have a wealth of first party data and a lot of it.

00:16:30.316 --> 00:16:34.196
Last I checked, our data lake stood at 117PB.

00:16:34.676 --> 00:16:40.476
We processed up to 300TB of data every day,
and we have more than 20 240,000 tables in our data lake.

00:16:42.436 --> 00:16:49.396
Speaking of platform and tooling as the first pillar,
using the base as is services like EKS,

00:16:50.676 --> 00:16:51.356
S3,

00:16:52.796 --> 00:16:54.636
EMR, and Aurora,

00:16:55.676 --> 00:17:00.716
we built the base platform and infrastructure
layer where we have compute for ML.

00:17:01.356 --> 00:17:06.396
We've got Trino, spark one and Starrocks
for our data compute and the data lake,

00:17:06.396 --> 00:17:12.796
which sits in S3. The next layer is the data transformation layer,
where for orchestration we use airflow.

00:17:13.316 --> 00:17:17.596
We have an in-house platform called slide built
on EMR for data integration.

00:17:18.316 --> 00:17:22.636
Chimaira again is our open source spark implementation
on ECS for data transformations.

00:17:22.636 --> 00:17:25.476
And then for data ingestion, we have a platform called Hugo.

00:17:26.076 --> 00:17:29.276
Next comes data management and governance.

00:17:29.996 --> 00:17:36.636
We have a data catalog platform called Hubble, which is a fork
of data hub where we store data, contracts, table metadata,

00:17:36.636 --> 00:17:39.756
any and all of that. We have an access control platform.

00:17:39.796 --> 00:17:43.116
We have a data observability platform which
checks in for completeness,

00:17:43.156 --> 00:17:45.436
data quality issues, things of that nature called Gen AI.

00:17:47.716 --> 00:17:52.316
And then we have our own implementation of data mesh called
signals Marketplace, which I’ll talk about more in an upcoming slide.

00:17:52.396 --> 00:18:00.276
Once we create these signals, we store them in our feature store
for serving and then in our metrics store for consumption,

00:18:00.276 --> 00:18:01.116
for analytics,

00:18:02.196 --> 00:18:06.996
for models, we have a model serving layer,
a training layer, and a model registry layer.

00:18:08.196 --> 00:18:14.036
And lastly, in the last couple of years, we've invested
heavily into AI tooling where we have a framework to build chatbots

00:18:14.076 --> 00:18:21.756
and Slack bots. We have an eval platform called
Eval Sub where we can eval all our agents.

00:18:21.796 --> 00:18:25.076
We made creation and deployment of front ends very easy.

00:18:25.116 --> 00:18:30.196
By providing front end components.
It's easy to apply guardrails and security as need be.

00:18:30.436 --> 00:18:36.876
And we have a unified AI gateway, which helps you access
all the frontier models via the central gateway at one go

00:18:38.236 --> 00:18:44.516
And then on top set all the applications which can use any and all
of these platforms and tools based on whatever you need to do.

00:18:45.116 --> 00:18:48.756
What this has done is it's made everybody a builder, every persona.

00:18:48.796 --> 00:18:53.076
Now at Grab is a builder. Be it a data scientist,
a product manager, an analyst.

00:18:53.796 --> 00:18:56.156
Engineers of course. And what we call data swarmers.

00:18:56.156 --> 00:18:58.036
Again, I'll talk about them in a bit.

00:18:58.276 --> 00:19:05.156
And then the solutions include agents like a merchant AI
assistant or MarketWatch, which is a marketplace management platform,

00:19:05.156 --> 00:19:11.156
or bricks, which is our creation and hosting Agentic platform,
which I'll delve into deeper in a bit.

00:19:12.876 --> 00:19:14.876
So let's talk about data practices now.

00:19:14.876 --> 00:19:19.756
So we've spent a lot of time and effort over
the last few years on quality and certification.

00:19:19.756 --> 00:19:25.196
We implemented our own version of data mesh called
signals Marketplace, where we defined ownership contracts,

00:19:25.196 --> 00:19:28.796
data quality checks for tables, metrics, and attributes alike.

00:19:29.316 --> 00:19:33.636
We institutionalized what we call data swarmers,
where for each business vertical,

00:19:33.676 --> 00:19:39.076
the engineers double up as data engineers taking
full control and ownership of their own data sets.

00:19:39.076 --> 00:19:45.996
And then we made data production incidents mainstream so that they get
the same level of importance as an operational incident would.

00:19:46.596 --> 00:19:48.996
Next up, context and skills. Relatively new,

00:19:49.196 --> 00:19:55.236
but we've already built a context store where each
of the verticals is responsible for publishing their context

00:19:55.756 --> 00:19:58.236
and to standardize a workflows and tasks.

00:19:58.236 --> 00:20:00.000
We are heavily leveraging skills.

